WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.900
How do processes communicate? They

2
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.740
can exchange messages or share

3
00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:06.640
a memory region, but both

4
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:08.520
approaches need a clear agreement

5
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.480
about who produces data and

6
00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:11.780
when it can be consumed.

7
00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:14.600
Concurrent execution alone does not

8
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:16.900
magically create a safe communication

9
00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:17.420
protocol.

10
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:20.120
With shared memory, both processes

11
00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:21.660
map an agreed region and

12
00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:24.160
exchange data there. Ordinary private

13
00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:25.660
memory after fork is not

14
00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:27.960
automatically that shared region. Once

15
00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:30.480
shared access exists, conflicting updates

16
00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:32.360
need synchronization so the consumer

17
00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:33.620
sees a consistent result.

18
00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:36.320
Message passing provides send and

19
00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:38.520
receive operations rather than requiring

20
00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:40.480
direct access to shared variables.

21
00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:43.000
Direct communication names the other

22
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.860
process. Indirect communication sends to

23
00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:47.720
a mailbox or port, so

24
00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:49.420
the communicating parties agree on

25
00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:50.360
that intermediary.

26
00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:53.220
A bounded mailbox has finite

27
00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:55.340
capacity. A send may have

28
00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:56.280
to wait when there is

29
00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:57.820
no free slot, and a

30
00:00:57.820 --> 00:00:59.000
receive may have to wait

31
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:00.200
when there is no message.

32
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:02.880
Whether an individual operation blocks

33
00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:04.260
is part of its specified

34
00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.380
semantics, not something you can

35
00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:07.640
guess from the word send.

36
00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:10.020
The lab uses pipes, which

37
00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:12.330
are unidirectional byte streams. Pipe

38
00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:14.260
endpoint zero is for reading,

39
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:16.260
and endpoint one is for

40
00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:18.340
writing. To send a request

41
00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:19.720
one way and a reply

42
00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:22.200
back, draw two pipes rather

43
00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:23.840
than quietly using a single

44
00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:25.040
one as if it were

45
00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:25.840
bidirectional.

46
00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:28.360
After fork, parent and child

47
00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:30.600
descriptor tables can contain references

48
00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:32.400
to the same underlying pipe

49
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.240
endpoints. Closing one descriptor removes

50
00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:37.600
that reference, not every reference

51
00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:39.700
in every process. The endpoint

52
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:41.380
can remain open while another

53
00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:43.360
descriptor still refers to it.

54
00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:45.440
For ping and pong, the

55
00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:47.140
parent keeps the request writer

56
00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:49.580
and reply reader. The child

57
00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:51.360
keeps the request reader and

58
00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.960
reply writer; each closes its

59
00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:56.760
unused ends. Animate ping from

60
00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:59.080
parent to child first, then

61
00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:00.500
pong back to the parent

62
00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:01.500
through the other pipe.

63
00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:03.620
A read reaches end of

64
00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:05.040
file only when no buffered

65
00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:06.880
data remains and all write-end

66
00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:09.200
references are closed. If the

67
00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.260
reader itself accidentally keeps an

68
00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:13.340
unused writer open, waiting for

69
00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:14.460
end of file may never

70
00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:16.560
finish. Merely closing the writer

71
00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:17.780
in the other process does

72
00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:19.360
not repair that extra reference.

73
00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:21.560
Check read and write return

74
00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:23.300
values and distinguish byte counts

75
00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:25.400
from safe text strings. A

76
00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:27.420
received sequence is not necessarily

77
00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:28.920
terminated as a C string,

78
00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:31.080
and a failed operation is

79
00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:32.940
not a successful empty message.

80
00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:35.260
The lab also requires closing

81
00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:37.220
descriptors and collecting the child

82
00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:37.900
appropriately.

83
00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:40.000
Things to take note of.

84
00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:42.900
Classify shared memory versus messages,

85
00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:45.140
direct versus mailbox naming, and

86
00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:47.580
bounded capacity versus operation blocking.

87
00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:49.980
For pipes, draw every descriptor

88
00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:51.520
reference after fork and close

89
00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:53.620
unused ends before reasoning about

90
00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:54.420
end of file.
